<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A sample that cannot be found ANYWHERE must not cost the user the whole
  project — and, since the MISSING PLACEHOLDER landed, must not cost the user
  the CLIPS ON IT either.

  THE HISTORY IS WORTH KNOWING, because this case has now gated three different
  behaviours over the same fixture:

    1. Originally a missing sample made instantiate() return NULL and the loader
       HARD-ABORTED the whole load. One unreachable file, no project.
    2. Then a failed instantiate was SKIPPED like an id-less element, and the
       no-progress leftover sweep DROPPED whatever referenced it. The project
       loaded — minus those clips, permanently once it was saved again.
    3. Now instantiate() returns a MISSING PLACEHOLDER: an SPlainWave that keeps
       the path exactly as the file spells it and the duration the project
       recorded, owns a source-less component, and renders SILENCE. Nothing is
       dropped, nothing is rewritten, and the reference resolves again by itself
       on a machine that has the file.

  Step 2 was not wrong for its time — it is much better than step 1 — but
  "the arrangement is quietly smaller than the user left it" is a bad thing for
  a DAW to do about a file that is merely somewhere else today.

  The fixture carries a GOOD track and an orphan BAD branch:

   1. track1 -> cut1 -> wave1 ("../test_sawtooth.wav") resolves via the qxa
      sample base dir and renders, exactly as in legacy_project_recovery.qxa.

   2. wave2 ("~/__smaragd_no_such_dir_missing__/__missing_sample__.wav") exists
      nowhere and its basename is NOT next to the project either, so basename
      recovery cannot save it. It becomes a placeholder; cut2, which windows it
      and is referenced by nothing, survives as an orphan exactly as it was
      written.

  WHAT EACH ASSERTION BITES. The audio assertions are unchanged and still catch
  a regression to step 1 (a hard abort leaves zero tracks and a silent render).
  `assert-extern-files` is the one that catches a regression to step 2, and it
  is the only kind of assertion that can: a missing sample is SILENT and so is a
  clip that was dropped for having one, so NO audio assertion anywhere can tell
  the placeholder from the drop. count="2"/missing="1" fails in both directions
  — a build that drops the file fails the count, and one that somehow loads it
  for real fails `missing`.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="sample_missing_survives">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <load-project path="../sample_missing_survives.qxp"/>

    <render filename="sample_missing_survives.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="2.5"/>

    <!-- 0-1 s: good track plays source sec1 (slip offset via startOffset=48000) -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="sample_missing_survives.wav"
                         startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.15" maxRms="0.21"/>

    <!-- 1-2 s: source sec2 -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="sample_missing_survives.wav"
                         startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         minRms="0.26" maxRms="0.33"/>
    <!-- BOTH waves are in the project: the good one, and the placeholder for
         the one this machine cannot see. In <actions>, not <assertions>: that
         block dispatches two hardcoded kinds and knows no verbs. -->
    <assert-extern-files count="2" missing="1"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
